  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "Which of the following transformations is correct?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x/y=x^2/y^2\\)",
    "B": "\\(x/y=(x+m)/(y+m)\\)",
    "C": "\\(x/y=xm/(ym)\\) (where \\(m\\) is any polynomial expression)",
    "D": "\\((x-y)/(y-x)=-1\\) (\\(x\\ne y\\))"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The basic properties of algebraic fractions only allow multiplying (or dividing) both the numerator and denominator by the same nonzero polynomial expression; adding the same expression is not allowed. Since \\(m\\) may be 0, C is incorrect. Since \\(y-x=-(x-y)\\), we have \\((x-y)/(y-x)=-1\\).",
